  "tldr": "Capacity-constrained treatment choice at a beyond-sample loss quantile is governed by the inverse of a selected heterogeneous survival mixture, not by separate cellwise extreme effects. For each fixed even cell count, the model is a nonempty Hall class and the data follow the observed marginal product experiment. The project derives the exact minimax expected logarithmic-regret frontier, identifies when capacity averaging strictly improves the coordinatewise Carpentier--Kim plug-in benchmark and why regular Athey--Wager complexity does not describe the tail loss, and constructs a computable sorting-and-inversion learner. A separate inference rung gives simultaneous policy intervals with selected-policy coverage as a corollary and attacks the sharp width converse through an explicit paired-cell Hall-splice hypercube.",
  "project_justification": {
    "gap": "Published quantile-policy methods supply fixed-level identification, one-tail Hall theory supplies a coordinatewise plug-in benchmark, and regular policy-learning theory supplies an additive-welfare complexity benchmark. None determines when capacity reallocation makes the exact heterogeneous mixture frontier strictly smaller than coordinatewise familywise control, nor the confidence-width modulus forced by paired Hall alternatives.",
    "niche": "For each fixed even cell count, the randomized product experiment identifies every policy mixture and the capacity optimizer is computable by sorting, while local tail-index ties and randomly empty cells retain a nonregular decision problem.",
    "fill": "The analysis combines a nonempty fixed-cell Hall class, exact soft mixture loss, and paired-splice testing to classify strict departures from both named regret baselines, then propagates bias-aware cell-tail regions through generalized inversion and matches them to a policy-mixture confidence-width converse."
